{"step_index":37,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-17T14:24:05Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\nReference david j meggi. Assessment of Musculoskeletal Disorders: a) Subjective Examination:\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nDemographic Data, Chief complaint, History of Present illness including functional limitation, Past Medical Surgical history, Family history, Occupational & Personal history, Socio-Economic, Environmental and Psychological History, Drug and Treatment History etc.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nb) Objective Examination: General Examination: Vital parameters.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n(Temperature, Respiratory Rate, Peripheral Pulses, Blood Pressure), Body Built of Patient, Attitude of Limb, Bony contours, Deformities, Trophic changes. Examination of Posture. Examination of Spasm, Tenderness, Edema /swelling/wasting including Limb girth measurement, Scar examination etc.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nMotor Examination: Range of Motion, Joint Play & End Feel, Muscle Strength, Power, Endurance, Flexibility, Isometric Resisted Testing, Limb-length discrepancy, Pelvic inclination, Examination of Balance, Gait & External aids/assistive Device used etc. Sensory Examination: Superficial & Deep sensation, Reflexes, Dermatomes, and Myotomes etc. c) Assessment of Hand Pinches, Grips, Routine sensory motor evaluation, Stereo gnosis etc.\r\n\r\nAssessment of musculoskeletal disorders relies on a structured, three-part evaluation protocol to systematically identify impairments, functional limitations, and underlying pathology.\r\n\r\nSubjective Examination\r\n\r\nDemographic Data: Age, gender, occupation, and dominant hand to establish baseline risks.\r\n\r\nChief Complaint (CC): Primary reason for seeking care, location, and onset of symptoms.\r\n\r\nHistory of Present Illness (HPI): Symptom behavior, aggravating/relieving factors, 24-hour cycle, mechanism of injury, and specific functional limitations.\r\n\r\nPast Medical/Surgical History: Prior injuries, surgeries, chronic conditions, and systemic diseases.\r\n\r\nFamily History: Hereditary connective tissue disorders, arthritis, or neuromuscular conditions.\r\n\r\nOccupational & Personal History: Ergonomic setup, daily physical demands, habits, and activity level.\r\n\r\nSocio-Economic, Environmental & Psychological History: Home setup, stairs, support system, stress levels, and fear-avoidance behaviors.\r\n\r\nDrug & Treatment History: Current medications (NSAIDs, corticosteroids, anticoagulants) and outcomes of past interventions.\r\n\r\nObjective Examination\r\n\r\nGeneral Examination & Vital Signs: Baseline temperature, respiratory rate, peripheral pulses, and blood pressure.\r\n\r\nVisual & Structural Observation: Patient body built, resting attitude of limbs, bony contours, overt deformities, and trophic changes (skin texture, hair loss, sweating alterations).\r\n\r\nPostural Assessment: Alignment across sagittal, frontal, and transverse planes.\r\n\r\nPalpation & Soft Tissue Evaluation: Identification of muscle spasm, localized tenderness, edema/swelling, muscle wasting, scar tissue mobility, and limb girth measurements.\r\n\r\nMotor Assessment:\r\n\r\nRange of Motion (ROM): Active (AROM) and passive (PROM) ranges.\r\n\r\nJoint Play & End Feel: Accessory motion testing and quality of resistance at terminal range.\r\n\r\nMuscle Performance: Manual Muscle Testing (MMT) for strength, power, endurance, and flexibility.\r\n\r\nIsometric Resisted Testing: Cyriax framework to differentiate contractile vs. non-contractile lesions.\r\n\r\nStructural Measures: True and apparent limb-length discrepancy (LLD), pelvic tilt/inclination.\r\n\r\nFunctional Mobility: Balance testing, gait cycle analysis, and evaluation with external assistive devices.\r\n\r\nSensory & Neurological Examination:\r\n\r\nSuperficial & Deep Sensation: Light touch, sharp/dull discrimination, proprioception, and vibration sense.\r\n\r\nReflexes & Segmental Testing: Deep tendon reflexes (DTRs), dermatomal light touch, and myotomal motor testing.\r\n\r\nHand Function & Specialized Motor Evaluation\r\n\r\nGrip & Pinch Strength: Standardized dynamometer measure for power grip, tip-to-tip, pad-to-pad (palmar), and lateral (key) pinches.\r\n\r\nRoutine Sensorimotor Function: Fine motor dexterity, rapid alternating movements, and coordination assays.\r\n\r\nStereognosis & Tactile Gnosis: Visual-occluded identification of familiar objects, functional hand orientation, and two-point discrimination.\r\n\r\nAssessment of Pain: Types of pain: Somatic, Somatic referred, Neurogenic, Visceral Subjective Assessment: Location, duration, progression, distribution, quality, diurnal variations, modifying factors, Severity, nature of pain, tissue irritability Objective Measurement & Documentation- Visual Analogue Scale (V.A.S), Numerical Rating Scale (N.R.S.), and McGill's modified questionnaire\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n(including Body Charts) etc.\r\n\r\nPain assessment systematically classifies pain characteristics, gathers detailed patient history, and quantifies intensity using standardized outcome measures.\r\n\r\nClassification & Types of Pain\r\n\r\nSomatic Pain: Originates from musculoskeletal structures (skin, fascia, muscles, joints, tendons, bone). Typically well-localized, aching, or sharp, and directly reproducible with movement or mechanical loading.\r\n\r\nSomatic Referred Pain: Pain perceived in a region distant from its actual musculoskeletal origin (e.g., gluteal pain originating from lumbar facet joints). It occurs due to convergence of nociceptive afferents on second-order neurons in the spinal cord. It is dull and aching, lacks dermatomal distribution, and does not involve neurological deficits.\r\n\r\nNeurogenic (Neuropathic) Pain: Caused by primary lesion or dysfunction in the peripheral or central nervous system. Characterized by burning, shooting, electric shock-like sensations, often accompanied by numbness, paresthesia, or allodynia in a dermatomal/peripheral nerve pattern.\r\n\r\nVisceral Pain: Originates from internal organs (e.g., heart, gallbladder, kidneys). Poorly localized, deep, cramping, or aching, and often accompanied by autonomic responses (nausea, sweating, pallor). Pain may refer to predictable somatic sites (e.g., left shoulder pain during myocardial infarction).\r\n\r\nSubjective Pain Assessment\r\n\r\nLocation & Distribution: Specific site of pain, mapped out precisely to identify whether it follows a localized, dermatomal, or visceral pattern.\r\n\r\nDuration & Progression: Onset (acute, subacute, chronic), sequence of development over time, and whether symptoms are improving, static, or worsening.\r\n\r\nQuality & Nature: Specific descriptive terms used by the patient (e.g., throbbing, burning, sharp, dull, aching, stabbing) to help differentiate tissue mechanisms.\r\n\r\nDiurnal Variations: Symptom fluctuations over a 24-hour cycle (e.g., morning stiffness in inflammatory arthritis vs. end-of-day pain in mechanical loading).\r\n\r\nModifying Factors: Specific activities, postures, movements, or interventions that aggravate or relieve symptoms.\r\n\r\nSeverity & Tissue Irritability: Degree of pain intensity combined with how easily symptoms are triggered (vigor of movement needed), how severe they get, and how long they take to subside (return to baseline).\r\n\r\nObjective Measurement & Documentation\r\n\r\nBody Charts: Visual anatomical maps where patients shade or mark pain locations using standard symbols (e.g., '#' for aching, '//' for sharp, '0' for numbness) to document distribution, depth, and spatial patterns.\r\n\r\nVisual Analogue Scale (VAS): A 100mm continuous horizontal line anchored by \"No Pain\" (0 mm) and \"Worst Possible Pain\" (100 mm). The patient marks a point, and the distance is measured in millimeters for precise tracking.\r\n\r\nNumerical Rating Scale (NRS): An 11-point numerical scale (0 to 10) where patients verbally rate their pain intensity, with 0 representing \"no pain\" and 10 representing \"worst pain imaginable.\"\r\n\r\nMcGill Pain Questionnaire (MPQ / Modified MPQ): A multidimensional rating tool evaluating sensory, affective, and evaluative dimensions of pain using grouped word descriptors, along with a present pain intensity (PPI) index.\r\n\r\nReference of Hutchison clinical-methods & Watchie .\r\n\r\nAssessment of Cardiovascular and Pulmonary dysfunction: Physical and functional evaluation of cardiovascular and pulmonary, normal and pathological conditions.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\na) Subjective Examination: Demographic Data. Chief complaint, History of\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nPresent illness including functional limitation, Past Medical Surgical history, Family history, Occupational & Personal history, Socio-Economic, Environmental and Psychological History, Drug and Treatment History etc.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nb) Objective Examination: Vital parameters (Temperature, Respiratory Rate,\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nPeripheral Pulses, Blood Pressure), Body Built of Patient, Breathing Pattern and breath hold test (rate, rhythm, use of accessory muscles), Attitude of Limb, Bony contours, Symmetry/Deformities of Head, Neck, Extremities, Trophic changes. Examination of cough, sputum, tactile and vocal fremitus, mobility of thoracic spine and rib cage, percussion etc. Auscultation: Normal and Abnormal Heart and breath sounds etc. Measurements: Chest Expansion, Symmetry of chest movement, BMI, Waist - Hip Ratio etc.\r\n\r\nCardiovascular and pulmonary assessment integrates subjective reporting with systematic physical examination to differentiate primary cardiopulmonary pathology from secondary systemic dysfunction.\r\n\r\nSubjective Examination\r\n\r\nDemographic Data: Age, sex, occupation, and home environment to identify primary risk factors (e.g., occupational dust exposure, cardiovascular risk profile).\r\n\r\nChief Complaint (CC): Cardinal cardiopulmonary symptoms including dyspnea, chest pain, cough, sputum production, wheezing, hemoptysis, palpitation, syncopal episodes, or peripheral edema.\r\n\r\nHistory of Present Illness (HPI) & Functional Limitations: Symptom onset (sudden vs. insidious), provocation/palliation, orthopnea, paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea (PND), and functional grading (e.g., NYHA Functional Classification or modified Medical Research Council [mMRC] Dyspnea Scale).\r\n\r\nPast Medical/Surgical History: Prior myocardial infarction, hypertension, asthma/COPD, rheumatic fever, cardiac catheterization, cardiothoracic surgeries, or prolonged immobilizations.\r\n\r\nFamily History: Early-onset coronary artery disease, cardiomyopathy, congenital heart disease, asthma, or cystic fibrosis.\r\n\r\nOccupational & Environmental History: Occupational inhalants (asbestos, silica, coal dust, organic molds) and home exposure (passive smoke, biomass fuel, environmental allergens).\r\n\r\nPersonal, Socio-Economic & Psychological History: Smoking history detailed in pack-years, alcohol and substance use, physical activity level, living conditions (stairs, ventilation), and illness-related anxiety or depression.\r\n\r\nDrug & Treatment History: Current cardiovascular and respiratory medications (e.g., beta-blockers, ACE inhibitors, diuretics, inhalers/nebulizers, oxygen therapy), dosage compliance, and past treatment responsiveness.\r\n\r\nObjective Examination\r\n\r\nVital Parameters:\r\n\r\nPulses: Rate, rhythm (regular vs. irregular), volume, character (e.g., pulsus paradoxus, pulsus alternans), peripheral vessel wall feel, and presence of radio-radial or radio-femoral delay.\r\n\r\nBlood Pressure: Resting systolic/diastolic, pulse pressure, postural changes (orthostatic hypotension), and pulsus paradoxus assessment.\r\n\r\nRespiratory Rate & Pattern: Normal resting rate (12–20 breaths/min), tachypnea, bradypnea, or abnormal rhythms (Cheyne-Stokes, Biot's, Kussmaul respiration).\r\n\r\nTemperature & Oxygen Saturation: Signs of systemic infection or baseline arterial hypoxemia.\r\n\r\nGeneral Inspection & Physical Build:\r\n\r\nBody Built & Nutritional Status: BMI, waist-to-hip ratio, cachexia (severe cardiac/pulmonary cachexia), or central obesity.\r\n\r\nPeripheral & Structural Signs: Cyanosis (central vs. peripheral), digital clubbing, jugular venous pressure (JVP) elevation and wave form, pedales/ankle edema, trophic skin changes, and stasis ulcerations.\r\n\r\nHead, Neck, and Limbs: Malar flush, xanthelasma, corneal arcus, nicotined-stained fingers, resting limb posture, and arterial supply/venous drainage integrity.\r\n\r\nThoracic Alignment & Structural Deformities:\r\n\r\nSymmetry & Bony Contours: Chest wall configuration (e.g., pectus excavatum, pectus carinatum, barrel chest), symmetry of scapulae, and thoracic spine mobility/deformities (scoliosis, kyphosis).\r\n\r\nAccessory Muscle Use & Breathing Mechanics: Hypertrophy/activation of sternocleidomastoids, scalenes, and abdominal muscles; intercostal/subcostal retractions; nasal flaring; and paradoxical abdominal movement (diaphragmatic fatigue).\r\n\r\nBreath-Hold Test: Qualitative index of ventilatory reserve and irritability.\r\n\r\nPalpation & Chest Wall Dynamics:\r\n\r\nTracheal & Cardiac Apical Impulse: Tracheal position (central vs. shifted) and apical impulse location, character (heaving, tapping), and presence of precordial thrills or parasternal lifts.\r\n\r\nChest Expansion & Symmetry: Measured upper, middle, and lower lobe excursion using a tape measure at specific anatomical landmarks (e.g., 4th intercostal space, xiphoid process).\r\n\r\nTactile / Vocal Fremitus: Transmission of spoken vibrations (\"ninety-nine\") across pulmonary segments (increased in consolidation; decreased in pleural effusion, pneumothorax, or emphysema).\r\n\r\nPalpable Spasm & Tenderness: Identification of rib cage/intercostal muscle tenderness or costochondral junction irritability.\r\n\r\nPercussion:\r\n\r\nAssessment of resonant (normal lung tissue), hyperresonant (pneumothorax, emphysema), dull (consolidation, atelectasis), or stony dull (pleural effusion) notes across corresponding thoracic segments.\r\n\r\nAuscultation:\r\n\r\nHeart Sounds: Normal S \r\n1\r\n\r\n  and S \r\n2\r\n\r\n ; presence of physiological/pathological splitting; extra sounds (S \r\n3\r\n\r\n  ventricular gallop, S \r\n4\r\n\r\n  atrial gallop); pericardial friction rubs; and cardiac murmurs (classified by timing, intensity, quality, and radiation).\r\n\r\nBreath Sounds: Normal vesicular, bronchial, and bronchovesicular sounds; diminished or absent breath sounds.\r\n\r\nAdventitious Sounds: Crackles/crepitations (early vs. late inspiratory, fine vs. coarse), wheezes (rhonchi), pleural friction rubs, and stridor.\r\n\r\nVocal Resonance: Transmitted voice sounds including bronchophony, egophony (\"E\" to \"A\" changes), and whispered pectoriloquy.\r\n\r\nAnswers the remains topic too\r\n\r\nCompleting the reference curriculum based on Hutchison’s Clinical Methods and Watchie’s Cardiovascular and Pulmonary Physical Therapy, below are the remaining core evaluation domains: Functional Capacity Evaluation & Exercise Tolerance Testing, Investigation & Diagnostic Test Interpretation, and ICU / Specialized Monitoring.\r\n\r\nFunctional Capacity Evaluation & Exercise Tolerance Testing\r\n\r\nField Walk Tests:\r\n\r\n6-Minute Walk Test (6MWT): Standardized submaximal exercise test measuring total distance walked in 6 minutes on a 30-meter track. Pre- and post-test assessment of HR, BP, SpO \r\n2\r\n\r\n , and Borg Rating of Perceived Exertion (RPE 6–20 or modified 0–10).\r\n\r\nIncremental Shuttle Walk Test (ISWT) & Endurance Shuttle Walk Test (ESWT): Externally paced field tests using audio signals to evaluate maximal functional capacity (ISWT) or submaximal endurance (ESWT).\r\n\r\nLaboratory Exercise Stress Testing (CPET):\r\n\r\nSymptom-Limited Exercise Testing: Performed on a treadmill (e.g., Bruce or Modified Bruce protocol) or cycle ergometer.\r\n\r\nParameters Measured: Continuous 12-lead ECG monitoring, blood pressure response, peak oxygen uptake (VO \r\n2\r\n\r\n  peak), minute ventilation (V \r\nE\r\n\r\n ), respiratory exchange ratio (RER), and anaerobic threshold (AT).\r\n\r\nTermination Criteria: ST-segment elevation/depression (>1 mm), sustained ventricular tachycardia, drop in systolic BP (>10 mmHg below baseline with increasing workload), moderate-to-severe angina, central cyanosis, or acute dyspnea.\r\n\r\nProtocol for cardiac exercise stress testing, AI generated\r\nProtocol for cardiac exercise stress testing. Source: Cleveland Clinic\r\nDiagnostic Investigations & Data Interpretation\r\n\r\nPulmonary Function Tests (PFTs):\r\n\r\nSpirometry: Forced Vital Capacity (FVC), Forced Expiratory Volume in 1 second (FEV \r\n1\r\n\r\n ), and FEV \r\n1\r\n\r\n /FVC ratio. Differentiates Obstructive (FEV \r\n1\r\n\r\n /FVC<70%) from Restrictive patterns (reduced FVC with normal/increased ratio).\r\n\r\nLung Volumes & Diffusing Capacity: Total Lung Capacity (TLC), Residual Volume (RV), and Diffusing Capacity of the Lung for Carbon Monoxide (DLCO).\r\n\r\nArterial Blood Gas (ABG) Analysis:\r\n\r\nEvaluation of pH (7.35–7.45), PaCO \r\n2\r\n\r\n  (35–45 mmHg), PaO \r\n2\r\n\r\n  (80–100 mmHg), HCO \r\n3\r\n−\r\n\r\n  (22–26 mEq/L), and base excess.\r\n\r\nDelineation of acute/chronic respiratory acidosis/alkalosis, metabolic acidosis/alkalosis, and hypoxemic vs. hypercapnic respiratory failure.\r\n\r\nElectrocardiogram (ECG) Analysis:\r\n\r\nRate, rhythm, axis determination, P-wave morphology, PR interval (0.12–0.20 s), QRS duration (<0.12 s), ST-segment alterations (ischemia/infarction), and T-wave inversions.\r\n\r\nStandard normal sinus rhythm ECG tracing, AI generated\r\nStandard normal sinus rhythm ECG tracing. Source: Scharvik / Getty Images\r\nRadiological & Imaging Modalities:\r\n\r\nChest X-Ray (CXR): Systematic evaluation using the A-B-C-D-E approach (Airway, Bones, Cardiac silhouette, Diaphragm, Expansion/Fields). Identifies consolidation, atelectasis, pneumothorax, pleural effusion, hyperinflation, and pulmonary edema.\r\n\r\nAdvanced Imaging: High-resolution CT (HRCT) for interstitial lung disease, CT Pulmonary Angiography (CTPA) for pulmonary embolism, and Echocardiography (Ejection Fraction, valvular integrity, wall motion abnormalities).\r\n\r\nLaboratory Biomarkers:\r\n\r\nCardiac Enzymes: Troponin I/T and CK-MB for myocardial injury.\r\n\r\nHeart Failure & Inflammatory Markers: B-type Natriuretic Peptide (BNP / NT-proBNP), C-reactive protein (CRP), D-dimer, arterial lactate, and complete blood counts (hemoglobin, leukocyte counts).\r\n\r\nICU & Specialized Monitoring Examination\r\n\r\nInvasive Hemodynamic Monitoring: Arterial line BP tracking, Central Venous Pressure (CVP: 2–8 mmHg), and Pulmonary Artery Cath (Swan-Ganz) parameters (PAWP, Cardiac Output, Cardiac Index).\r\n\r\nMechanical Ventilation & Airway Assessment: Endotracheal/tracheostomy tube placement, ventilation modes (AC, SIMV, PSV), tidal volume (V \r\nt\r\n\r\n ), PEEP, FiO \r\n2\r\n\r\n , peak airway pressure, and static compliance.\r\n\r\nSecretions & Airway Clearance: Quantity, color, viscosity, and odor of aspirated/expectorated sputum using standardized grading scales.\n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-09-17T19:54:05+05:30.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>"}
